# Two Kings Chess: Netlify. Publish the folder `node scripts/build-web.mjs --host netlify` writes,
# which holds the site and nothing else. Netlify has no rule that serves only a list of paths, so
# should a checkout be published instead, these keep the repository out of the site by name:
# every top-level entry it tracks, the folders git, npm and Expo leave beside them, the usual
# .env files, and Apache's config, which a --host netlify build leaves out anyway.
# src/__tests__/website.test.ts runs every tracked file through these rules, so an entry added
# to the repository fails it until it is listed here. The ! makes a rule apply even where a file
# exists (Netlify serves an existing file over a rule without it). Netlify serves dotfiles;
# .well-known/ is the one folder of them that belongs to the site. The site's own assets are
# under /assets/assets/, which neither the repository's two asset folders nor /assets/:file (one
# segment, the files at the top of assets/) reach. Cloudflare Pages has no 404 rules: build for
# it with --host cloudflare, which keeps only its own config.
/.htaccess                /404.html  404!
/.git/*                   /404.html  404!
/.github/*                /404.html  404!
/.claude/*                /404.html  404!
/.expo/*                  /404.html  404!
/.editorconfig            /404.html  404!
/.gitignore               /404.html  404!
